# Headless WordPress SEO

WordPress is the CMS source of truth for blog SEO metadata. Astro owns frontend rendering and `<head>` output. Do not inject WordPress-generated HTML into Astro’s `<head>`. Do not add RankMath-specific code unless the project switches SEO plugins.

## API source

- Fetch posts from `/wp-json/wp/v2/posts`.
- Use `yoast_head_json` as the primary Yoast SEO source.
- Do not scrape/parse `yoast_head`.
- Do not re-implement `_yoast_wpseo_*` fields when `yoast_head_json` is available.

## Map `yoast_head_json` → Astro `<head>`

| Yoast field | Output |
|---|---|
| `title` | `<title>` |
| `description` | `meta[name="description"]` |
| `robots` | `meta[name="robots"]` (preserve `index`, `follow`, `max-snippet`, `max-image-preview`, `max-video-preview`) |
| `canonical` | `<link rel="canonical">` |
| `og_title` / `og_description` / `og_url` / `og_type` / `og_image` | Open Graph tags |
| `twitter_card` / `twitter_title` / `twitter_description` / `twitter_image` | Twitter tags |
| `schema` | JSON-LD |

- No duplicate SEO tags.
- Prefer Yoast’s schema graph; do not rebuild Article/BlogPosting when `schema` exists.
- Render schema as JSON-LD only — one graph, no duplicates.

## Fallbacks (only when Yoast value missing)

- Title → post title
- Description → excerpt
- OG image → featured image
- Canonical → Astro canonical URL for the post

Do not invent SEO values WordPress already provides.

## URLs & architecture

- Blog URLs: `/blogs/{slug}/` (keep production-compatible so Yoast canonicals stay valid).
- Centralize SEO rendering in one shared Astro SEO/head component.
- Blog pages pass WordPress SEO data into that component; do not duplicate SEO logic per page.
- Keep the implementation minimal; no unnecessary dependencies.
